Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time the Muslims came to Jesus. And they asked him, "Who is the most important person in the Kingdom of Heaven?"
Jesus took a little child and took him to himself, and commanded him to take his place among some of them.
Jesus said, "What I am about to tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you don't do that, you will not enter the Kingdom of Heaven."
Whoever becomes free from such slavery as this child represents is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Whoever accepts such a little child in my name accepts me.
But what if someone causes one of these little ones who believe in me to sin? It would be better for him to have a large millstone tied around his neck and to be thrown into the depths of the sea.
How close to the world will it be because of the things that drive people to sin! Things of this kind must come. But how close to those who cause it will it be!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one hand or foot than to enter hell with two hands and two feet. In hell, the fire burns forever.
If your eye causes you to stumble, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with only one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into the fire of hell. The spiritual journey of the enlightened souls.
Look, if you want to get on top of one of these little things, that's what you've got to do. That's what's been revealed to you. And you deserve to see yourselves in the heavens, and you deserve to see my Father who is in the heavens.
<.>
What do you think? Let's assume a man owns 100 cows, and one of them collapses. Will he leave 99 cows on the mountain? Will he go and look for the one that got away?
What I am going to tell you is a fact. If he finds those sheep, he is happier to have them than he was with the 99 that were not lost.
This is the will of your Father who is in heaven. He does not want any of these little ones to be lost. When someone sins against you
If your brother commits an offense against you, go to him and tell him what wrong he has done. Keep it between yourselves. And if he listens to you, you will have triumphed over him.
But what if they will not listen to you? Take one or two more with you. The scripture says: "At the mouth of two or three witnesses every matter will be established." (Deuteronomy 19:15)
But what if he refuses to even hear the witnesses? Then report that to the congregation. And what if he refuses to even hear the congregation? Then you are to treat him like a person outside the congregation or a tax collector.
What I will tell you now is true. What you close on earth will pass into heaven. What you open on earth will pass into heaven.
Again, this is what I tell you. For example, if two of you on earth agree on anything you ask for, my Father in heaven will do it for you.
"I am with them if they gather together in my name from two or three." The messenger whose offenses are not forgiven to those who wronged them.
Peter came to Jesus and said, "Lord, how often shall I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The kingdom of heaven is like a certain king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.
As the king began to do so, a man potentially carrying millions of dollars was connected to him.
The man was unable to pay, so the Usurer ordered him to be sold, along with his wife, his sons, and all that he possessed, in order to pay off what he owed.
The slave fell at his feet, saying, "Give me time, I will repay everything."
Its owner felt sorry for it, forgave it for what it had been charged with, and allowed it to go out.
But then this worker went and found one of the other servants who owed him a few dollars, so he took him and began to squeeze his chest and said: "Pay back what you owe me!"
The other server fell on his leg. He said, "Give me time, I will repay you."
But the first master refused. So he went and had the man sentenced to prison. And he will be kept until what was owed is paid back.
The other servants saw what happened. They were very disturbed by it. So they went and told their man everything that happened.
Then the Bailiff summoned the First Servant and said, "You are a bad servant." He replied, "I have forgiven you all the debts you owed me because you asked me to."
Why didn't you have mercy on yourself on the other server as you had mercy on me?
In anger, his owner returned him to the prisoners. He will be punished until he returns everything he owes.
Thus shall every one of you father in the heavens, so long as you do not forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
A large number of people followed him, and he healed them there.
Some of the Pharisees came to test him. And they asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any reason?"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read that from the beginning the Creator made them male and female?" (Genesis 1:27)
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and be joined to his wife, and they shall become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
It was not as it had been, but they became one. So it is not for a man to disperse what God has brought together.
They asked: "So then, why did Moses command that a man should send away his wife with a bill of divorce and send her away from him?"
Jesus answered, "Moses gave you permission to divorce your wives because your hearts were hard. But it was not like that from the beginning."
Here is what I tell you. Anyone who divorces his wife and then marries another woman commits adultery. A man is only allowed to divorce his wife if she is not committed to him.
The Muslims said to him: "If this is the case between a husband and wife, it is better not to take a wife."
Jesus answered, "Not all people can accept the idea of remaining in a state of marriage. Only those who have been helped to live without marriage can accept that."
Some men cannot be born children because they were born this way. And some were made this way by other people. And others were founded this way in order to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever can accept to live this way should do so. "Children are seated to Jesus"
Some people brought their little children to Jesus, hoping he would place his hands on the children and pray for them. But the teachers told the people to go away.
Jesus said, "Suffer the little children, and forbid them not, to come unto me: for of such is the kingdom of heaven."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he moved on from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man came to Jesus and said, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to have eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "For there is only one who is good. If you want to enter into the kingdom, then follow the commandments."
"Which?" the man asked. And Jesus said, "You shall not murder. You shall not commit adultery. You shall not steal. You shall not bear false witness."
Honor your parents. (Deuteronomy 20:12-16; Exodus 5:16-20) And "love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
"I obeyed all those commands," the young man said. "What should I do after that?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Jesus said to his workers, "What I am about to tell you is true. It is hard for the rich to enter the kingdom of heaven."
I tell you again: it is very hard for a camel to go through the eye of a needle. But it is easier for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God.
When the Muslims heard this, they were truly shaken. And they asked, "Who then can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "What is impossible for people is possible for God."
Peter answered him: "We have kept everything for your triumph! What reward will you receive?"
"What I am now going to tell you is the truth," Jesus said to them. "When all things are being created anew, the Son of Man will sit down upon His bright throne. Then those who follow Me shall go forth to thrones which are called twenty. They shall go forth to rule twenty times ten of Israel."
Everyone who has left homes, families, or lands because of me will receive ten times as much, and they will also receive eternal life.
But many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The story of the workers in the field.
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who owned a piece of land. He went out early in the morning to hire workers to work in his vineyard.
I agree that they should be paid the usual amount for a day's work. Then send them to his farm.
At nine o'clock in the morning, he went out again. He saw others standing in the market square doing nothing.
He told them, "Go also and work in my vineyard, and I will pay you what is right."
So go. "He went again around noon and at three o'clock, repeating the same thing."
At five o'clock, he went out and found others standing around him. He asked them, "Why have you stayed here all day doing nothing?"
"Because no one has hired us," they answered. And he said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyard."
When night came, the owner of the garden spoke with the person in charge of the workers, and said, "Inform the workers and pay them their wages. Begin with those who were taken captive last, and then proceed to the first of them."
The newly hired workers were sent out around 5 o'clock. Each of them received the usual amount for a day's work.
So, when those initially hired came, they expected to receive a larger sum. However, each of them received the usual amount for an hour of work.
Once they took possession, they began to criticize the owner.
"The people you recently hired worked only an hour," they said. "You paid them the same amount you paid us. We completed most of the work and spent a full day under the hot sun."
One of the owners answered him: "Friend, I am being fair with you. Did you not agree to work for the usual hourly wage?"
Get your money out and go. I want to pay the same amount I paid you to those I sent to last time.
Do I not have the right to do what I want with my own money? Do you feel you were misled because I freely gave to others?
So whoever is in the rear will be in the front, and whoever is in the front will be in the rear.' Jesus speaks again about his coming death.
Jesus was heading to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the 12 disciples aside to talk to them.
"We are going to Jerusalem," he said. "The Son of Man will be delivered to the chief priests and the scribes. They will condemn him to death."
A mother asks Jesus for help.
Behdini's father came to Jesus, and his sons followed him. She knelt at his feet and asked him for something.
"What do you want?" Jesus asked. "I want you to tell me which of my sons will sit at your right hand in your kingdom," she said. "And I want you to tell me that the other son will sit at your left hand."
"You do not know what you are asking," Jesus said to them. "Can you drink the cup of suffering that I am to drink?" "Yes, we can," they answered.
Jesus said to them, "You will drink from my cup. And yet, it is not for me to say who will sit at my right hand or at my left hand. These places are prepared for those whom the Father has prepared them for."
Ten other Muslim women listened to that. And they were overcome with anger towards the two brothers.
He took their Jesus by the council, and said, "You know who delivers the kingship in the provinces. They possess the people of their command. And they command them their works the new ones."
Do not be like that. Rather, anyone who wishes to be considered important among you must be his slave.
And anyone who wants to be first must be his slave.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give his life as a ransom for many people. Two blind men were sitting, and they were resting because of the health of their eyes.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A large crowd followed him.
There were two blind men sitting by the road. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they called out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!"
The crowd ordered them to stop, and asked them to be quiet, but the two men shouted even more, saying: "Oh Lord, my son David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called them to him and said, "What do you want me to do for you?"
"Our God," they answered, "we want to be able to see."
Jesus was asked about them with deep concern, so he touched their eyes and they were healed immediately, then they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
As they all approached Jerusalem, they came to the house of Fatima. It was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two of the disciples.
He told them, "Go to the village ahead of you. When you reach it, you will find a cow tied up, with its calf with it. Untie the rope and take its burden to me."
If anyone tells you something, say: "The king needs it." The owner will send it off immediately.
This happened so that what was heard from the Messenger would be fulfilled. And it says:
Say to Jerusalem, "Behold, your king is coming to you; righteous and having salvation is he, humble and riding on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey." (Zechariah 9:9)
The Muslims did what Jesus had commanded them to do.
They brought out the donkey and the child, and they placed on each of them their clothes. Then Jesus sat on the clothes.
A large mass of people were thrown onto the road, and others cut down trees from the trees and placed them on the road.
Some people were taken before him, and others behind him. And they all doubted, saying, "This is Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "This is Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city shook. The people asked, "Who is this?"
The people answered, "This is Jesus. He is the messenger from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus is expelled from his mosque.
Jesus entered the area of the altar and began to drive out everyone who was buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of those who exchanged money, and he also overturned the seats where they were selling birds.
He said to them: "It is written in the book that the Lord said: 'I will call My house a mosque of those who offer praises.' (Job 56:7) But you are making it a mosque for thieves.'" (Yerub 7:11)
The crippled and maimed people came to Jesus in the mosque, and he healed them there.
The older rulers and the scholars of the law saw the wonderful things he did. They saw the children in the temple area shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" But when they saw all this, they were indignant.
Do you hear what these children are saying? He said to them. Yes, Jesus replied. Have you never read about that in the Scriptures? For it is written: " 'My heart rejoices in the Lord; my strength in him. For he looks on the humble; he cares for the lowly in heart. He himself, though mighty, takes notice of humble servants. From heaven he looks down and sees all the children of men. He sees those who are humble in spirit, and he knows those who are not proud. For though the humble may be scorned by the proud, the Lord sees and knows, and he will give justice to the humble and peace to those who are oppressed. The wicked, though they boast of great strength, cannot even save themselves from destruction. When God brings judgment against his enemies, he will give them over to those who hate them. Though his enemies may boast of their strength, they will be brought down to destruction. When God brings judgment against his enemies, he will give them over to those who hate them. Though they boast of their strength, they will be brought down to destruction. When God brings judgment against his enemies, he will give them over to those who hate them. Though they boast of their strength, they will be brought down to destruction. When God brings judgment against his enemies, he will give them over to those who hate them. Though they boast of their strength, they will be brought down to destruction. When God brings judgment against his enemies, he will give them over to those who hate them. Though they boast of their strength, they will be brought down to destruction. When God brings judgment against his enemies, he will give them over to those who hate them. Though they boast of their strength, they will be brought down to destruction. When God brings judgment against his enemies, he will give them over to those who hate them. Though they boast of their strength, they will be brought down to destruction. When God brings judgment against his enemies, he will give them over to th